data: {"delta":{"partial_json":"{\"file_path\": \"/tmp/mission_summary.md\", \"content\": \"# Mission summary: greet, tested\\n\\n## Outcome: campaign-met\\n\\nAll three stated criteria are satisfied, verified independently (not just from\\ndev's report):\\n\\n1. `src/greet.py` exists on dev's branch (`cs-sandbox/<DEV:1>.<GROUP:1>`,\\n   commit `4cf0fb0`) with `greet(name)` returning `f\\\"Hello, {name}!\\\"`.\\n2. `tests/test_greet.py` exists, using `unittest`, covering:\\n   - a normal name (`greet(\\\"World\\\") == \\\"Hello, World!\\\"`)\\n   - the empty string (`greet(\\\"\\\") == \\\"Hello, !\\\"`)\\n3. Independently checked out dev's branch into a fresh worktree\\n   (`/tmp/verify_subject`, detached at `4cf0fb0`) and ran\\n   `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests` myself: output was\\n   `Ran 2 tests in 0.000s / OK`, exit status 0.\\n\\n## Process\\n\\n- Dispatched the task to `dev` (dev/d002) with the exact mission requirements.\\n- dev implemented both files, ran the suite, committed to its own branch, and\\n  replied with commit hash and test output.\\n- I fetched dev's branch (`cs-campaign-member fetch dev`), confirmed\\n  `treeDiffersFromBase: true`, read both files directly out of the branch via\\n  `git show`, and re-ran the required test command myself in an isolated\\n  worktree rather than trusting the reported output.\\n- Accepted dev's reply (`cs-campaign-member accept dev`) after this\\n  independent verification.\\n\\nNo unmet items.\\n\"}","type":"input_json_delta"},"index":1,"type":"content_block_delta"}

